WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.060
Welcome to another edition of the RF Video Shoot Interview Series.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.320
First of all, I'd like to thank Jim Cornette for having us in this house.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.080
I'd like to thank you for being had.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.080
That's great.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.800
Of course, you've been everywhere in the world of professional wrestling,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.690
from Mid-South to Texas, to NWA, to the WWF, WCW.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.870
You started your own promotion, Smoky Mountain Wrestling.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.690
And currently you work with the World Wrestling Federation,

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.210
and you're also booking, I believe, Ohio Valley Wrestling?

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.780
Yep.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.770
Actually, I supervise the training program,

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.000
the developmental program that they have here in OVW.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.050
There's a couple of different developmental territories, and it was kind of my idea really

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.530
to do something like that anyway, and when I met Danny Davis, we were talking earlier

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.350
before we got on camera.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.000
I've known Danny for 20 years.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.090
He and Ken Wayne were the first tag team that I managed, and Danny's a fabulous worker,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.310
and he's also an honest guy, and I just happened to run into him when I was visiting home a

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:54.400
couple of years ago, and he asked me to come and see the school, and I said, you know,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.650
this might be a way I could move out of Connecticut.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.000
So I took the opportunity to do so.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.360
My first question would be, how do you get started in the wrestling business?

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.610
I started when I was like 9 or 10 years old.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.250
I started watching wrestling on television like everybody did at that time.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.930
You know, the Memphis show with Lance Russell and Dave Brown.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.500
And I started going to the matches here at the Louisville Gardens.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.240
And after a couple of years, I started taking pictures just for myself, you know, just because I just wanted to have pictures.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:20.540
Right.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.920
Well, I started getting kind of good at it.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.000
I didn't know anything about photography.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.600
I just wanted to take wrestling pictures.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.080
I didn't give a shit about weddings or whatever.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.540
The guys started seeing the stuff, and they said, hey.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.700
could sell those and so they started buying some from me then I started doing the programs and the

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.420
pictures and ring announcing and you know I was me and Bobby Eaton were the two classic hangers on

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.960
in the wrestling business you know he was putting up the ring I was ring announcing you know the

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.100
same same principle and just anything I'd do to be close to the business and close to the guys and

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:52.290
just around it did you want to wrestle early on um yeah well you know I everybody wants to wrestle

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.520
everybody in the business wants to wrestle around the business but realistically I could not see

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.000
myself uh you know being beat the shit out of you know on a

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.070
regular basis so I always thought well maybe I could manage or I could just you know do promos

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:09.960
in a car you know that was my only goal I didn't actually think I'd do this for money and then one

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.380
day I was I was taking pictures at Memphis TV and I'm trying to get a shot and all of a sudden

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.760
somebody taps me on the shoulder I turn around to Jerry Jarrett I said oh god I'm in trouble oh what

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.300
have I done I didn't know I'd done anything but I figured I had to be in trouble why would he talk

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.280
to me right he said can you come back here I said oh god he wanted me to be a manager he said you

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.000
know we've been you know Jim we've known you for six years I started when I was 14 doing

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.970
that stuff and he said um you know you've been around for so long and i understand you do a heck

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:41.190
of a promo just in the back you know i'd imitate terry funk or whatever and um he said you want to

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:46.010
think about it for a week i said no uh yeah i mean yeah no yeah yeah no i'll do i'll be here i'll be

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.370
here come to tv next week i'm in a business all of a sudden i said jesus christ i don't even

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:56.170
remember the trip home it's 400 miles i was on neptune i'm gonna do the wrestling business so

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:00.000
anyway and the next week i went oh and i told there's i

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.360
I didn't want to tell anybody kayfabe.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.320
I mean, I wasn't even smart yet, and I was kayfabing.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:07.540
But I told one friend of mine here, I said, just watch TV next week.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.620
You know, because it was a week delay, right?

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.400
So right before I left, I said, watch TV next week.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.140
There's going to be something really special going on.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.700
I get there, and, you know, the Memphis show was 90 minutes,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.460
and then on the bicycle around the other town, it was 60 minutes.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.020
I got cut.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:20.920
They ran over.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:21.940
So I was black in Memphis.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:22.720
They didn't see it up here.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:23.520
So he watches the show.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.420
He says, what was supposed to be so great?

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:26.940
Watch next week.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.100
Finally, I got on, you know.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.000
So then he saw

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.060
I thought he was hot because I didn't tell him.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.590
What was the first Saw Promo like in front of the movie?

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.590
Murderous.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.590
Horrible.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.950
Everybody said it was good, but I guess it was good for being the first one, but when

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:43.290
I look at it now, I wish that I could burn all the existing tapes, and you don't have

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.290
it, do you?

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.290
Absolutely.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.310
And you ain't gonna get it.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:47.310
What did you do?

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:52.760
I came out and, see, Jerry Jarrett wanted me to do the old Gary Hart deal, which before

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.470
he became Playboy Gary Hart, and then the bald-headed Gary Hart that, you know, he was

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:00.000
a mama's boy, rich kid from Chicago, millionaire Playboy who got...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.000
everything he wanted had all this money so he said oh god he's trying to cut Gary Hart and shoot

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.770
Gary Hart and stab Gary Hart he had a ton of heat and I said this is not unattractive and so you

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:13.670
know I came out and said well I've decided I want to be a manager but I don't want to manage guys

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:19.050
like Jimmy Hart does because you know they're common and uncouth I want to manage Jerry Lawler

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:24.020
and Bill Dundee and all the the popular wrestlers and well Lance you know as interviewing me he was

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.260
great he feeds you the lines right he just dragged me along I had a hook in the mouth

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.000
Well, what type of work have you been in before?

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.330
Jimmy, I said, well, as I said, I, you know, all the people had seen me taking pictures,

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:37.190
you know, at the matches, so I said, I took pictures.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.370
Well, but, I mean, how did you support yourself?

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.950
Oh, well, Vance, you know, my family is very wealthy, but I didn't need any money.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:47.070
I mean, my God, I don't even concern myself with such matters, you know, I started getting

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:51.260
a little cocky there and automatically everybody's going, who is this dumb shit and why is he

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:52.260
on our TV?

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.040
And I had heat from day one because I was such a putz.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.040
Right.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.040
I didn't even know what I was doing.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.000
Dundee told me one time, if we ever smart you up, you'll kill your gimmick because you'll

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.620
no heat, but gradually I was able to make the transition where hopefully I knew what

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.340
I was doing and I still piss people off, which I do to this day, ask anybody who lives with

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.340
me.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:14.050
Well, I was going to ask you, what are your early memories of working with Lawler?

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.630
Lawler is, I always loved Lawler's work and his talent.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:24.310
I mean, Jerry Lawler has done things with promos and with psychology and just the bumps

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.310
he took at one time, I mean, he's 50 years old now, so, you know, give the guy a break.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:29.860
He was one of the best bumpers in the business.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:35.360
But he was a master of psychology, and I think that's what I picked up on early was

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.880
you didn't need to be Mr. Muscles and Johnny Athlete.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.860
You needed to be able to touch people's head, and that's the same thing that Eddie Graham did,

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.180
and Eddie Graham taught Bill Watts, that's the same thing Watts did,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.680
and all the great bookers and all the great talents in the business.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.720
They were always masters of psychology, and it doesn't matter the perfectly executed springboard moonsault.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.550
If you know when to kick it in like a crazy man and when to back up like a chicken shit,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.500
And you've got 20,000 people in the building, all totally different individuals, same basic

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:06.260
human emotions.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:08.320
You get a master of psychology and you draw money.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:09.270
All right.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.720
Who was your biggest mentor back then?

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.060
Really, actually, from watching Lawler, that was where I picked up a lot of just how to

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.180
be natural on camera, I hope.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.680
But to be honest, Bill Dundee was one of the masters of finishes.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.000
I've still got my books from Louisiana every night.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.000
See, this is the thing that guys don't do in the business.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.680
business anymore. Every night I would write down what the town was, what our match was, who we

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.910
wrestled, what the finish was, what the house was, what I got paid. It's a habit. And I got 82, 83,

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.790
84, 85, 86, 87, 88. And most of those are Dundee's finishes are the ones that I stole from Dundee

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.650
and took them to places he hadn't been yet, you know, or got in in front of him. And, you know,

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.570
he told me one time, he said, you've made more money with my finishes than I have, you know,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:58.590
but hey, he knew how to get people up and down. It wasn't just, we went to Dallas when we first

00:06:58.590 --> 00:06:59.700
got to world class.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.760
And me and Bobby and Dennis were like, okay, we're going to work with the Fantastics and we're going to tear down the house, have a great match.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:05.520
What do we do for a finish?

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:12.410
Ken Mantell comes in and says, what about if when Tommy Rogers is picking Bobby up for a slam, Dennis kicks him?

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.210
That was a hot – and we're like, what the hell?

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.070
Because we're used to doing these Bill Watts five-minute extravaganzas.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.430
We did that, and the place came apart.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:23.990
We had to go out the side door where they'd take the heels when they thought they were going to get attacked.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.220
Come to find out, the boys couldn't remember complicated finishes, the Von Eriks.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.000
So they didn't give them to anybody's.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.600
So the people, it was like a virgin territory.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.500
If you'd have done anything like they were doing in Louisiana, you'd have had a riot.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:39.610
But that's, so we adapted because why devalue what you do?

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.780
If they don't require the triple moonsault, don't do the triple moonsault.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.600
If they're going to pop on the first one, don't do three flips.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:48.410
So we kind of backed up a little bit.

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.030
We still had good matches, you know, just different style.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:51.310
Right.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.500
Back in Memphis, who did you travel with?

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.840
For the first six months or so, I was in a business as he takes a drink.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:00.940
Both of us.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:04.550
For the first six months, I still lived here, and nobody lived in Louisville.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.340
So I basically traveled myself.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:11.010
Every Friday, I'd get in a car 420 miles to Tupelo for $50.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:15.470
Then 110 miles over to Memphis that night to do TV the next morning,

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:18.010
to do 200 miles back up to Nashville to come, you know, blah, blah, blah.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:18.350
Right.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:21.100
So finally I broke down and moved to Nashville,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.000
and then I used to ride with Frank Morrell, the angel,

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:27.300
and I rode with Bobby Eaton a lot, and Adrian Street and Miss Linda,

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.210
who were hilarious.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:28.710
Do you know them?

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:30.000
Yeah, I don't know them personally, but I don't know them.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.860
hilarious to ride with, my stomach would hurt when I'd get out of the car from laughing

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.400
at them, especially when they'd start arguing with each other.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:40.760
Because Adrian was classic, and he had that accent, and he did all the cockney slam and

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:41.760
everything.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:44.320
And Linda's all demure, but Linda was the boss, but Adrian didn't know it, and Linda

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:45.440
didn't either.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.810
But if Adrian didn't have Linda around, he wouldn't know how to find his hat.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:50.810
So they were funny.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.440
Just, you know, guys like that, and you'd be able to pick up a lot of stuff.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.040
I mean, I used to ride with some of the guys before I got into business, because like Bobby

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:59.920
Fulton, when he worked this territory.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:03.170
He'd ride over to Evansville every Wednesday with us because my mom would...

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.290
See, I started before I had a driver's license.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:05.610
Right.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:07.120
So my mom would have to drive me.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:08.500
So she'd sell the gimmicks.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.040
She'd sell the pictures I was taking while I was announcing her taking pictures or whatever.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:15.950
And Bobby Fulton and Troy Graham, the dream machine, who was the funniest son of a bitch in the world.

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:17.070
It was insane.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:21.840
But, you know, they would ride with us because, A, they didn't have to pay for gas,

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:25.630
and, B, Bobby finally, he was going to sell his car that he had here for 50 bucks,

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.010
but then the guy couldn't come up with the money, so he just left it on the side of the road.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:29.330
That's where his car was at at that point.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:30.000
So...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.760
We'd give him a ride, and he'd tell us stories about being in Calgary and working for Stu Hart,

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.840
and maybe he could be the best Stu Hart in the entire world.

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:38.920
Everybody does that impression.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:39.540
That's great.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:43.660
That's one thing the guys don't get a chance to do anymore is riding a car because that's where you learned everything

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.580
and not going to the town because then it was all bullshit stories.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:50.060
But coming back, it was like, now we're going to critique these sumbitches we worked with.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:50.600
Right, right.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.300
And then you'd figure out what was going on.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:57.480
I guess, who were some of the early guys you managed early on?

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.000
Well, the way they started –

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.180
started me out, I was like, I was this screw-up manager that, you know, couldn't hit the ground

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:09.240
with my hat. Right. So for the first week, I managed Sherry Martell because the girls were

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.880
just in. Right. So they said, let's put coordinate with the girls. And I'm so happy. My mommy's

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.570
bought me a girl. And then the second week was Dutch Mantell, which I think we went two weeks

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:22.870
with that because my first time managing in Memphis, I'm in a main event, Dutch Mantell

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:27.390
versus Jerry Lawler in the Mid-South Coliseum. And I'm standing there going, I don't know what.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:30.000
I'm just waiting for when I'm speaking

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:34.590
to get in here and I'm scared I'm in a main event for Christ's sake and I haven't even been at

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:41.080
ringside before and with the girls I just did the TV so you know and then I've somehow cost Dutch

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:44.420
the match and the next week he comes out and fires me on TV so then I'm going to get even with him

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:49.470
so I get Crusher Broomfield one man gang right and I set his career back five years and he'd only

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:53.950
been in business three at the time I mean they oh Jesus Christ 400 pounds and he could move like

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:59.340
crazy and as soon as I started managing him he couldn't beat the popcorn guy then was um

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:05.500
I think Jesse, Jesse Barr, God bless Jesse, and then was Adrian and Linda.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.720
Adrian and Linda was the first time I actually did something that actually threw money and

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:12.980
we had an angle, we had a program, and he and Dundee went like 13 straight weeks in all

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:17.300
the towns, Memphis and Louisville and Nashville, and we did the strap matches and the lumberjack

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:21.640
matches and the handicap matches, three of us against Dundee, and he, as a matter of

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:25.840
fact, thank you, Bill, right here, 12 stitches all the way down to the bone in Memphis one

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:25.960
night.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.470
Now, he threw me out of the ring, but he threw me so hard, and coincidentally, this happened

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:30.000
to have been...

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:32.780
match and the first time anybody had thrown me out of the ring, and nobody bothered to

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.430
tell me they were going to do it ahead of time, so I don't know how I am.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:39.430
He threw me so hard I hit the middle rope, which bowed out, snapped me back, I went upside

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:43.880
down, side of the face across the edge of the ring apron, which in Memphis, by the way,

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:50.110
is never padded, bare boards, and I land on the concrete like that, and it's a lumberjack

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:53.520
strap match where Terry Taylor, Stan Lane, Steve Kern, and Lawler round the ring with

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:54.520
belts.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.960
When you get thrown out of the ring, they're going to whip you until you get back in, and

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:00.000
they were taking liberties anyway, plus some mark in the front row had handed Kern

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.860
belt with these big studs on it so he was double fisting the thing they're just messing with us

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:10.000
so I land there and I look up and I said I'm conscious I can't believe that and all of a sudden

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:14.280
I saw this red shit right about here and Terry Taylor come over and he draws the belt back he's

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:22.640
and turn around and left and I said oh my finger went in it I was like oh this is and it's 10 more

